```java
// 2Sum.java

public class Solution {
    public int[] twoSum(int[] numbers, int target) {
        // Create a HashMap to store the numbers we've seen so far and their indices.
        java.util.HashMap<Integer, Integer> numToIndex = new java.util.HashMap<>();

        // Iterate over the array of numbers
        for (int i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
            int complement = target - numbers[i];

            // Check if we've seen the complement before
            if (numToIndex.containsKey(complement)) {
                // If we have, return the indices of the two numbers
                return new int[] {numToIndex.get(complement), i};
            }

            // Otherwise, add the current number and its index to the HashMap
            numToIndex.put(numbers[i], i);
        }

        // If no solution is found, return null
        return null;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution solution = new Solution();
        int[] numbers = {2, 7, 11, 15};
        int target = 9;

        int[] result = solution.twoSum(numbers, target);

        if (result != null) {
            System.out.println("The indices of the two numbers that add up to " + target + " are: " + java.util.Arrays.toString(result));
        } else {
            System.out.println("No solution found");
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses a HashMap to store the numbers we've seen so far and their indices. It iterates over the array of numbers, calculates the complement (the number that would add up to the target sum), and checks if we've seen the complement before by looking it up in the HashMap. If we have, it returns the indices of the two numbers; otherwise, it adds the current number and its index to the HashMap.
